[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-GRAMMAR-FROM-NIX
TITLE: Generate the grammar from a typed Nix option surface, and gate it
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Generate a typed Nix option surface from strictdoc's own grammar definition,
and generate docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra from that surface. The surface is derived,
not hand-written: nothing about what a grammar may contain is typed by a
person.

Two layers. The first is faithful -- it expresses exactly what a .sgra file can
express, with no opinion of ours in it. The second is normalized: named
converters turn faithful shapes into better Nix types, so a regex that is a
pure literal alternation becomes an enum, a two-value alternation becomes a
boolean, an optional group becomes a nullable with a null default. A shape no
converter recognises is an error rather than a fallback to free text, so an
upstream grammar change fails during the update sweep instead of reaching
anyone. Declaring a value unconstrained is itself a converter, keeping "we
decided this is free text" distinguishable from "we did not recognise this".

The extractor runs at generation time, outside Nix, and emits Nix source that
is committed; Nix never parses strictdoc during evaluation. A drift check
regenerates and diffs, so a stale surface is a red check.

How the conversion is expressed is open. Two shapes, both viable, and the
choice decides whether the faithful layer is a committed file.

Converters can live inside the extractor, working on the parsed grammar. That
preserves why each shape was classified as it was: a pattern is known to have
come from a particular grammar rule rather than inferred from how it rendered.

Or the extractor emits the faithful surface and a declarative rewrite
transforms it. Measured: ast-grep is tree-sitter based and ships a Nix grammar,
so it parses our shapes with nothing added; a rule of about fifteen lines turns
a literal-alternation pattern into an enum; and the fail-closed condition is
one more catch-all rule at error severity rather than a special case in code.
Both the tool and its Python bindings are packaged already, and the bindings
work on Nix source in process, so this needs no second toolchain and no
separate file format -- the rewrite can live in the same script as the
extraction.

The remaining cost of the second shape is provenance, and it is the whole
argument. It matches generated text rather than the grammar behind it, so a
two-value alternation that means a boolean is indistinguishable from one that
merely looks like it.

Our own grammar values -- the five node types with their fields and relations
-- are data filling the generated surface, not part of it.

Add the validation gate: a clean-directory strictdoc export over the whole
corpus at commit time and in CI. This is not a second opinion. None of
strictdoc's grammar checks run on a bare parse, so the corpus export is the
only validation that ever happens to a generated grammar.

What belongs at evaluation is only what strictdoc has no rule for anywhere: two
fields with the same title, and two relations with the same type and role.
Both are made unrepresentable by keying on name rather than asserted against.
Everything strictdoc does enforce is left to strictdoc and becomes a negative
fixture proving the gate fires.

Consumer surface the operator reviews before implementation: the normalized
option tree and the converter list. Reviewed after: the regenerated grammar as
an empty diff, the gate firing on a deliberately broken fixture, and an
unrecognised grammar shape failing the extractor.

Not included: behaviour, transitions, ownership rules, which fields count as
contract, which node types a relation may point at, and any ordering policy of
ours. Those are the semantic layer, defined by a consumer of this surface
rather than by it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Generating rather than hand-writing is the operator's call, and the reason is
drift: the overlay now tracks a project shipping roughly three releases a
month, and a hand-written surface does not notice a knob being added. It keeps
generating a valid grammar that cannot express the new thing, with nothing
going red.

Measured, and it is derivable: strictdoc assembles its own grammar into a
string that is byte-identical across the last two releases; a metamodel built
from it reaches 25 of 43 rules from the root, and its abstract rules yield the
four field types and three relation types without a list being typed by hand --
including that file relations have no reverse role at all. The metamodel alone
is not sufficient, reporting every attribute as mandatory and flattening value
vocabularies, so optionality and literal sets come from the parser tree beneath
it. Both are reachable from the same object.

Also measured, and the reason two checks stay at evaluation: a grammar
declaring one field twice and one relation twice exports clean with exit zero,
and the duplicates resolve in opposite directions -- fields keep the last
declaration, relations keep the first. No rule against either exists anywhere
in strictdoc.

Carried from the spike: a typed surface regenerates the grammar byte-for-byte
in 0.03 s and rejects thirteen of thirteen bad declarations once the merged
value is forced; strictdoc rejects six of six injected grammar defects; the
gate needs nothing but the strictdoc package; and cross-field checks must live
in one place that forces evaluation of the whole value, because Nix's addCheck
never runs on a nested option.
<<<
PARENT_FP: >>>
DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP:0000000
DEC-FP-FIELDS-CONFIGURABLE:0000000
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The faithful layer carries every native grammar feature by construction, so
nothing needs listing as a deliberate inclusion. What the NORMALIZED layer
exposes is a scoping decision and is open below.

What the implementation settled, each of which had been left open here:

Element order is carried by the VALUES. Elements, fields and relations are all
lists, never attribute sets, because Nix sorts attribute-set keys and strictdoc
enforces order -- so the conceptual order survives and no reshuffle was needed.
The consequence is that duplicate detection is an explicit assertion rather than
a structural side effect, which is why the two duplicate checks exist at all.

Both layers are materialised as committed files, and the component is general
purpose: the normalized layer carries every native grammar feature, including
the composite nodes, view styles, human titles, child relations and file roles
this repository never uses. Those are exercised by
packages/strictdoc-grammar/fixtures/foreign.nix, which round-trips
byte-identically, and the five node types are one consumer in
packages/strictdoc-grammar/values.nix rather than part of the surface.

Two corrections the extraction needed, both found by running the chain:

A '^'-anchored negative lookahead is POSITIONAL, not a value constraint.
FieldName is /(?!^UID)(?!^RELATIONS).../ and arpeggio matches with re.MULTILINE
at the current position, so the lookahead can only fire at a line start -- which
a field title, sitting after the literal '  - TITLE: ', never is. Hoisting those
two into a value-level denial produced a type that rejected a field named UID,
which this repository's own grammar declares five times. They are now recorded
under denyAtLineStart and not enforced.

The choice vocabulary could not keep its upstream name. textx calls it options,
and a submodule whose CONFIG carries a key named options is read by the Nix
module system as an option DECLARATION, so every sibling key errors out. The
extractor now renames reserved module-system attribute names, and a collision
with no rename is fatal rather than silent.

WHAT CLOSES THIS SLICE, ruled by the operator 2026-08-26. The slice is
implemented but not accepted, and these three stand between:

  MECH-STRICTDOC-DEVENV-MODULE -- an ai.strictdoc module owning the wrapping and
    the grammar generation, with enable, package and grammars.
  MECH-GRAMMAR-EXTRACT-SOURCE-BOUNDARY -- strip the wrapped environment out of
    the overlay; the overlay converts, the module wraps.
  MECH-STRICTDOC-UPSTREAM-FLAKE -- take strictdoc from upstream's own flake and
    retire the step-zero overlay, accepting that it tracks main.

Settled at the same time and no longer open here: docs/sdoc/grammar.sgra IS
generated, through that module, as this repository's convention rather than a
constraint on consumers. Contract-bearing fields are semantic and their edge
leaves this slice -- see MECH-CONTRACT-FIELDS-EDGE-TO-SEMANTIC-LAYER; the
PARENT_FP entry moves with it and only the operator changes those. HTML
rendering becomes a requirement of its own rather than a property of this
slice, tracked as MECH-HTML-RENDER-MUST-PASS.

Still needing a round with the operator before they can be closed: whether the
grammar gates belong in the flake checks output, whether the emitter should stay
reachable without the type in front of it, and whether the line-anchored
denials recorded above are enforced anywhere. None of the three blocks the
three closure items above.
<<<
