[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-BEHAVIOUR-MODEL
TITLE: Define the behaviour model in Nix and render it for review
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Define, as a typed Nix option tree layered over the grammar:

- the lifecycle of each node type: its states over grammar fields, the legal
  moves, who may make each move, what each move writes;
- triggers: a command, an outside event such as landing in main, a timer,
  another move finishing;
- gates and invariants, each declaring what it needs to see, so that the
  places it can run are derived rather than declared;
- relation contracts: which node types a role may point at, whether cycles
  are allowed, whether a change propagates across the relation;
- flows.

Cross-check every value against the grammar at evaluation. From one
evaluation emit a machine-readable model for the tool and the validators, a
state diagram per node type, a diagram per flow, a table of moves against
gates, a table of gates against where they can run, and a report of what is
still only prose.

Consumer surface the operator reviews before implementation: the option tree
and the rendered diagrams -- this is where the node lifecycle becomes
something to look at rather than guess at. Three shape questions are open and
are brought one at a time before the surface is fixed: whether state is one
machine per field or one machine over all of a node's state fields at once;
whether the ripple -- what goes stale when a node changes -- is derived from
declared read and write scopes or from a per-field contract flag; whether a
flow is a list of move references or a step-by-step script. Reviewed after:
the diagrams and tables regenerate from one evaluation, and the rendered
lifecycle matches the graph as it stands.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Every part of this was prototyped in the spike and its review: both state
shapes, four representations of gate placement that all derive the same
result -- including that the authorship guard can run nowhere today -- flows
that drift when they restate moves, and eight rendered views from one
evaluation.
<<<
PARENT_FP: >>>
DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP:0000000
DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY:0000000
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Vocabulary adopted from the outside research: a command is a request and may
be refused; an event is an accepted fact; a milestone is an achieved fact that
can go stale; a gate's evidence records what it depended on. The instance's
signed edge is carried as evidence that can go stale rather than as a node
type of its own -- contested in review, settled when the surface is fixed.
<<<
